`age-enough` already exists, and data-pile already vendored it by hand

You asked that anything shaped like family work get flagged rather than carried in
someone's head. This is the first one the shell count turned up, and it is the
sharpest example in the whole family argument: **it is the failure the extraction
exists to prevent, already committed.**

Documentation only. No code moves, no decision is made.

## What is there

`composer/` holds a working age implementation — `age-mint.mjs`, `age-seal.mjs`,
`age-open.mjs`, `chacha20poly1305.mjs` — and `age-mint.test.mjs` asserts WebCrypto
derives the same recipient the real `age-keygen -y` does. That is the family's health
test, met against the actual tool, already.

`FCCN-ANTIBODY/data-pile` needed it and there was no address to point at. So it
**inlined the code**, and its own headers say so plainly:

    bin/age-open.mjs    "the age battery (anecdote.channel/composer/age-seal.mjs + age-mint.mjs), inlined"
    bin/age-keygen.mjs  "A trimmed slice of anecdote.channel/composer/age-mint.mjs"

Two copies, cross-referencing each other in comments, with nothing that fails when
they drift.

**This is not the `yaml-enough` case.** There are no rival implementations to
reconcile — one implementation and a hand-cut slice of it. So `age-enough` needs no
merge: extract, mount, delete the inline copy. Possibly the cheapest member after
`jekyll-enough`.

One detail I enjoyed: `data-pile/bin/prove.mjs` calls it *"the vendored age battery,"*
and `git-enough/workflow.mjs` names the shell gap as needing *"a JS battery."* The same
word, reached independently, for a thing with no address.

## It has already paid for itself and nobody noticed

Of the 13 `hosted-only` steps in #235's count, **five are `sudo apt-get install age`**:

| workflow | installs `age` for | state |
|---|---|---|
| `ingest.yml` | `bin/ingest` | **nothing.** `bin/ingest` and `bin/lib.sh` make zero `age` calls |
| `report.yml` | `bin/ingest`, `bin/report` | **nothing.** Same |
| `prove.yml` | `bin/prove` (bash), shells to `age -d` | replaceable — `bin/prove.mjs` is a byte-compatible port, cross-verified against the bash bin in `test/prove.test.mjs` |
| `setup.yml` | `age-keygen` | replaceable — `bin/age-keygen.mjs` |
| `test.yml` | `test/run.sh`, fixtures from the real tool | **keep.** Making a fixture with the tool and asserting the port reads it is how parity is proven |

Two vestigial, two replaceable by JS that is already written and already tested, one
load-bearing. **That is a deletion and two path changes, not a build** — which is what
#235 meant by *the cheapest way to shrink this gap is not to build anything.*

Verified rather than inferred: I grepped `bin/ingest`, `bin/report` and `bin/lib.sh`
for `age` invocations and there are none; `bin/report` mentions `bin/decrypt` only in a
comment describing an extension point.

## Whose call it is

**`data-pile`'s**, for the workflows. I have not filed anything there and have not
touched it. It is recorded here because the family boundary is defined here, and
because the inlining is evidence about **this repository's missing address** rather
than about their judgement — they did the right thing with the options they had, which
is exactly the argument for giving these things addresses.

Also flagged in the order, and deliberately left unplaced in it: `age-enough` needs no
merge and has a proven consumer, so it could reasonably go second — but it is
`composer/`'s code rather than `git-enough/`'s, and whether the family takes members
from outside that folder is a question the list has not had to answer yet. That one is
yours.

## Verified

`node scripts/test.mjs` → 118/118. Prose-only change; nothing executable moved.
